nice try ...

consider this: NetHack, although it knows its own needs, has
historically relied on some external object for definition.
Unix, naturally, was the first crutch.  ANSI-compliance would
be another external crutch.  to be radical, NetHack should
simply define all of its needs, in effect, creating a virtual
toolbox or interface.  _any_ platform wishing to support NetHack
would then merely have to understand NetHack's requirements and
implement them in a natural way for that platform.  poof! no
reliance on Unix, ANSI-compliance, etc.  keep in mind that i'm
assuming that NetHack's toolbox is prototypical (i.e. makes few
(and commonly understood) assumptions about file I/O, display
output and command input).  this would allow NetHack to be
played on Unix with a dumb terminal and on your favorite
personal computer/workstation with wonder graphics and the
ultimate in user-friendly input interface.  best of all, if
properly done, you could play _your_ game on any convenient
NetHack engine (assuming equivalent versions) since the
platform is irrelevant ...

